Subject: Planning memo — transmission network design to connect dispersed generators and consumer load centers

Context and objective
Our utility is planning a new transmission network to move power from multiple generator sites to geographically distributed consumer nodes. We need to decide which transmission lines to build and how to route power so that all consumer demands are met, generator outputs stay within their limits, per-line transfer capacities are respected, and the overall lifecycle cost is minimized. That lifecycle cost includes both the capital cost of building lines (primarily a function of distance) and the operational “heating” cost caused by resistive losses that grow with the square of the power flow.

Available inputs
- Node locations (map coordinates) are provided in {NODE_COORDINATES}
- Node roles (generator or consumer) are listed in {NODE_TYPES}
- Consumer power requirements are given in {POWER_DEMANDS}
- Generator maximum outputs are specified in {GENERATOR_CAPACITIES}
- Copper conductor material properties: resistivity is specified in {WIRE_RESISTIVITY} and cross-sectional area in {WIRE_CROSS_SECTION}
- Planned operating voltage for the network is set in {OPERATING_VOLTAGE}
- Per-line maximum power transfer is limited by {LINE_CAPACITY}
- Construction cost per kilometer is defined in {BUILD_COST_PER_KM}
- The monetary cost rate applied to resistive losses is given by {LOSS_COST_RATE}

Design considerations
- Line siting and construction cost: Building a line between two nodes costs money proportional to its length, so longer routes carry higher capital expenditure. Distances are determined by the provided map coordinates, and the total build cost will scale with the number and length of lines selected.
- Resistive losses and heating costs: Once a line is energized, the operational cost we pay for losses is driven by physics rather than a simple linear rate on flow. Resistive losses follow a square law (I²R), meaning the loss is proportional to the line resistance times the square of current. In power terms, that translates to a cost proportional to Resistance × (Power / Voltage)². Doubling the power flow on a given line quadruples the heating losses and thus the loss cost. This strong nonlinearity is central to the planning decision; we cannot just sum flows and apply a linear penalty without distorting the economics.
- Copper conductor resistance: For a given material, resistance increases with line length and decreases with conductor thickness. With copper, the resistance of a candidate corridor will rise with distance and fall with larger cross-sectional area. As a result, longer lines and thinner conductors drive up I²R losses, while thicker conductors and shorter routes mitigate them.
- Role of operating voltage: Higher voltage reduces current for the same transmitted power, making losses drop with a 1/V² effect. Operating at the specified voltage materially lowers the heating cost on each line; this factor must be explicitly captured in the loss cost model rather than approximated away.
- Capacity limits and feasibility: Power can flow only on lines we choose to build, and each built line must respect the specified maximum transfer limit. Generators cannot exceed their capacity, and each consumer must receive enough power to meet demand.
- Planning treatment of losses: For grid planning, we use a lossless nodal balance. Power is conserved at the nodes; losses are modeled as a separate financial penalty applied to the flows rather than a physical deduction from delivered energy. This approach keeps the network feasibility clear (generators supply exactly what consumers require) while still reflecting the true cost implications of resistive heating.

Decision to be made
- Select the set of corridors to construct between nodes and determine the power flow on each built line. The selection must balance:
  - Capital cost growth with route length and number of lines
  - Operational heating costs that rise quadratically with per-line flow and with resistance
  - The mitigating impact of operating at high voltage on those losses
  - Physical transfer limits per line, and generator and load requirements at the nodes

Planning goal
Minimize the total cost of the network: the sum of construction cost and the cost of resistive losses. The loss cost must be computed using the physics-based square law (Resistance × (Power / Voltage)²) rather than any linear approximation. This will naturally favor designs that avoid concentrating too much power on a single corridor, consider shorter routes where practical, leverage conductor geometry to reduce resistance, and make full use of operating at high voltage to suppress I²R losses—while still meeting all consumer demands and respecting generator and line capacity constraints.
